# Everything the image does not need. Keeping this tight matters twice over:
# a smaller build context is faster, and nothing secret can reach a layer by
# accident.

# Dependencies and build output — reinstalled and rebuilt inside the image
node_modules
.next
out

# Secrets and local configuration. `.env.example` is deliberately NOT ignored:
# it is documentation. Real values arrive at `docker run`, never in a layer.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Version control and CI
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode
.idea
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Docker's own files
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml

# Staging area for files removed before release — never part of a build
_release-cleanup

# Logs and scratch
*.log
npm-debug.log*
coverage
tmp/
temp/

# src/digest-server/ and digest-schema/ are deliberately NOT ignored, and this
# is the one entry worth reading twice.
#
# One .dockerignore serves both Dockerfiles. Dockerfile.digest is built from
# this same root context and needs the contract, which is the whole reason the
# processor's old vendored copy could be deleted. And this image needs them too
# now: the build stage runs `npm test`, which includes the processor's own suite
# and scripts/noWritePath.test.ts. Ignoring them would turn both into a silent
# skip, so the dashboard image would stop proving that it has no write path at
# exactly the moment that proof started mattering.
#
# They reach no layer of this image. The build stage copies the tree, but the
# runtime stage copies only .next/standalone, .next/static and public, so the
# processor is present where it is tested and absent where it would run.
#
# BuildKit does support a per-Dockerfile ignore file
# (Dockerfile.digest.dockerignore) which would let this one stay tight. Not
# used on purpose: without BuildKit it is silently ignored, and the first
# symptom is the processor build failing on a missing contract — a confusing
# way to discover which builder you are on.
